{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the ass turned aside from the road, etc.</b> In the Tanchuma (Balak 8) they said: He showed him the signs of the patriarchs, etc. For 'and she walked into the field' is a wide place, to say that if he wished to attack the seed of Abraham, he would find room to curse the children of Keturah, and Ishmael. And if he wished to curse the seed of Isaac, he would find one side to curse, for he could bend toward Esau. But from the side of Jacob, whose bed was whole, it stood in a narrow place, for from his side 'there was no way to turn right or left.' And there are yet other approaches among the commentators." "And from what Rashi writes, 'this — three festivals,' you wish to uproot a nation that celebrates three festivals (Numbers Rabbah 20:14) — hear from this that those three signs he mentioned correspond to the three festivals, and he begins from Sukkot, which is in the month of Tishrei, Rosh HaShanah, of which it is said (Deuteronomy 16:13): 'When you gather in your produce from the field,' and concerning it was said: 'And the ass turned aside from the road and walked into the field' — you wish to uproot a nation that celebrates the festival of gathering, which depends on the field, and on it Israel offer seventy bulls corresponding to the seventy nations, and if so, their nullification causes the nullification of all the nations." "And afterward he said 'in a path of the vineyards' — an allusion to the festival of Pesach, the season of the exodus from Egypt, of which it is said (Psalms 80:9): 'You plucked up a vine from Egypt; You drove out nations and planted it' — for justice demands uprooting the thorns and planting the vine, and how do you wish to reverse the order and uproot the vineyards because of the thorns? And the nature of the vine is that it is trampled underfoot, yet in the end the wine fells the one who tramples it when he drinks it — as is concluded in the Yalkut, parashat Vayeshev (146), in the name of Midrash Shocher Tov, and there it brings many comparisons about Israel being likened to a vine, all of which serve to nullify the intent of Balaam. And it alludes to the festival of Pesach, on which they drink four cups of wine corresponding to the cup of salvation for Israel, who are likened to a vine, and four tongues of redemption are written, and corresponding to them the Holy One, blessed be He, makes the nations drink four cups of reeling, as is concluded in Bereshit Rabbah (Vayeshev 88:5) — and how do you wish to uproot all this and reverse the order between Israel and the peoples?" "And afterward he said 'and she stood in a narrow place where there was no way to turn right or left' — corresponding to Atzeret, on which they received the Torah, of which it is said (Proverbs 3:16): 'Length of days is in her right hand, in her left hand riches and honor' — and had Israel not received the Torah, the world would have returned to chaos and void (Shabbat 88a), and all the nations would have been nullified. And how do you wish to uproot this nation that celebrates this festival, which is needed by all the nations and by all that exist above and below? And it is possible that also 'in a path of the vineyards' he alluded: you wish to uproot a nation that celebrates the festival of Pesach, on which they drink four cups, corresponding to the four kingdoms, and of wine, in the number seventy corresponding to the seventy nations — and this nullification also causes the nullification of all seventy nations." "And it is possible to explain further this 'three festivals' to which he alluded: how do you wish to uproot a nation that upholds the three things on which the world stands (Avot 1:2) — that is, the three legs of the world, and they are: Torah, divine service, and acts of kindness — for their nullification is the destruction of the entire world. And he alluded to acts of kindness with what he said 'and she walked into the field,' for the essence of acts of kindness comes from the field, by means of priestly gifts, tithes, gleaning, forgotten sheaves, and the corner. And the divine service in the Temple, which is likened to a vineyard, he alluded to with 'in a path of the vineyards' — and thus Rashi explained 'He brought me to the house of wine' (Song 2:4): this is the Tent of Meeting; and concerning them it is said (Song 7:12–13): 'Come, my beloved, let us go forth into the field, let us lodge in the villages, let us go early to the vineyards,' etc. And 'in a narrow place where there was no way to turn right or left' alludes to the Torah, in the manner already explained, and this is a correct allusion."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase 'סִימָנֵי אָבוֹת הֶרְאָה לוֹ' is a headline-summary of the midrash in Tanchuma; the body of the comment then unpacks it.", 1 "Resolved the abbreviation 'במד״ר' as Numbers Rabbah per the citation convention; chapter and verse follow standard print editions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:46.734801996Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}